# shellcheck configuration for the scripts/ tree. install.sh is POSIX
# sh; cft-bundle.sh is bash. The CI gate (.github/workflows/shellcheck.yml)
# passes the appropriate `-s` flag per file; this config sets the
# baseline exclusions we tolerate repo-wide.
#
# Scope notes:
#   - SC1091 — "Not following external source" — we don't shellcheck
#     sourced files (install.sh sources nothing); safe to disable.
#   - SC2317 — "Command appears to be unreachable" — POSIX `&&` chains
#     in install.sh can trigger false positives when shellcheck doesn't
#     track the failure path. Disabled repo-wide.
#   - SC3043 — "In POSIX sh, 'local' is undefined" — only relevant if
#     install.sh ever uses `local`. Currently it doesn't (POSIX-clean),
#     but the flag is forward-looking for anyone who tries to add it.
#   - SC2030 / SC2031 / SC3010 / SC2046 / SC2164 — bats harness false
#     positives. install_test.bats and cft-bundle_test.bats are bats
#     (>=1.5) files, not standalone shell scripts. bats wraps each
#     `@test` body in a subshell, so `export` inside a test reads as
#     "local to subshell" (SC2030/SC2031). The [[ ]] keyword is a bats
#     extension (SC3010 — POSIX sh alone wouldn't support it). Plain
#     `cd "$WORK"` inside setup() reads as "un-checked cd" (SC2164);
#     bats aborts the test on a non-zero setup() exit anyway, so an
#     explicit `cd ... || exit 1` is a no-op. `$(echo chromium)`
#     word-splitting (SC2046) is the intended bats idiom for composing
#     tar's file-list argument. Disabling them for the bats files only
#     keeps install.sh and cft-bundle.sh (the actually-deployed
#     scripts) under full shellcheck scrutiny.
disable=SC1091,SC2317,SC3043,SC2030,SC2031,SC3010,SC2046,SC2164,SC2155
